---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="ScreenRecorder recording settings"
  description="Choose a ScreenRecorder resolution, frame rate, and power profile for presentations, tutorials, or higher detail."
>
  <article class="guide wrap">
    <a class="back-link" href="/docs/">← All documentation</a><div
      class="eyebrow"
    >
      CAPTURE · 4 MIN READ
    </div><h1>
      Choose the right<br /><span class="accent-word">recording profile.</span>
    </h1><p class="guide-lead">
      Start with the smallest profile that keeps the details your audience
      needs. Higher resolution and frame rates increase storage and can use more
      power.
    </p><h2>Resolution presets</h2><div class="guide-table">
      <table>
        <thead
          ><tr
            ><th scope="col">Preset</th><th scope="col">Output size</th><th
              scope="col">A good starting point for</th
            ></tr
          ></thead
        ><tbody
          ><tr
            ><th scope="row">360p</th><td>640 × 360</td><td
              >Quick notes and small text-free demos</td
            ></tr
          ><tr
            ><th scope="row">480p</th><td>848 × 480</td><td
              >Lectures and general screen sharing</td
            ></tr
          ><tr
            ><th scope="row">720p</th><td>1280 × 720</td><td
              >Readable tutorials and walkthroughs</td
            ></tr
          ><tr
            ><th scope="row">1080p</th><td>1920 × 1080</td><td
              >Fine detail and high-quality presentations</td
            ></tr
          ></tbody>
      </table>
    </div><h2>Frame rate</h2><p>
      30 FPS is a useful default for desktop applications, slides, and
      tutorials. Higher frame rates can make fast motion smoother, with greater
      encoding, battery, and storage cost.
    </p><h2>On battery</h2><p>
      On Windows, 360p and 480p use existing power-saving encoder caps while on
      battery. The 720p and 1080p profiles preserve the selected quality and may
      use more battery. Fedora has an efficiency mode that is automatically
      clamped on battery; its High quality profile is an explicit opt-in.
    </p><h2>Hardware encoding</h2><p>
      The Windows build prefers Intel Quick Sync H.264 when available and falls
      back to software encoding when needed. Fedora prefers Intel VA-API
      encoding and has additional fallbacks. Which encoder is selected depends
      on your system and installed support.
    </p><div class="guide-callout">
      <span>ⓘ</span><p>
        These profiles are choices, not promises of a fixed file size or battery
        runtime. Encoder behavior and on-screen content affect both.
      </p>
    </div><a class="text-link guide-next" href="/docs/audio-camera/"
      >Next: audio and camera →</a>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
